WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.093 --> 00:00:09.663
FAYE MOSS: Hello, everyone! Welcome back to Superstars in History , the talk show featuring famous people from

2
00:00:09.664 --> 00:00:15.625
the past. Today's guest is one of the most beloved and glamorous movie stars of all

3
00:00:15.626 --> 00:00:26.535
time. She's an EGOT, a rare individual who's won every major American entertainment award: Emmy, Grammy, Oscar, and Tony.

4
00:00:26.536 --> 00:00:32.123
And she's equally famous for her humanitarian work on behalf of the world's children. Straight

5
00:00:32.124 --> 00:00:53.320
from history, please welcome Audrey Hepburn! AUDREY HEPBURN:

6
00:00:53.321 --> 00:00:55.242
Hello, Faye.

7
00:00:55.243 --> 00:01:00.105
FAYE MOSS: I'm so excited to finally meet you! I'm such a

8
00:01:00.106 --> 00:01:06.989
huge fan. I know you were born in Belgium in 1929, but I don't know anything about your family.

9
00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:12.753
AUDREY HEPBURN: My mother was my main influence because my father left us when I was

10
00:01:12.754 --> 00:01:20.384
six. She was a Dutch baroness and had wanted to study opera, but her family disapproved of a career on the stage.

11
00:01:20.385 --> 00:01:25.550
So she put all her creative energy into encouraging my interest in ballet. I

12
00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:31.291
had two half brothers, Alexander and Ian, from my mother's first marriage. They were

13
00:01:31.292 --> 00:01:35.207
much older than I, but we were very close. FAYE MOSS:

14
00:01:35.208 --> 00:01:40.164
You grew up to be an actress—you must have been an outgoing child. AUDREY

15
00:01:40.165 --> 00:01:43.600
HEPBURN: On the contrary, I was quite shy.

16
00:01:43.601 --> 00:01:52.087
My mother sent me away to boarding school in England when I was only five years old, thinking it would make me more outgoing. When

17
00:01:52.088 --> 00:01:58.946
World War II started in 1939, she took me to the Netherlands, where her family lived. She thought

18
00:01:58.947 --> 00:02:07.144
we'd be safer there because Great Britain and Nazi Germany were at war, and the Netherlands was a neutral country. But we were still

19
00:02:07.145 --> 00:02:11.888
in danger because we lived near the German border. My mother warned me

20
00:02:11.889 --> 00:02:20.732
never to speak English in public for fear that German soldiers might overhear me and think I was British. FAYE MOSS: I learned in history

21
00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:26.067
class that being neutral didn't help the Netherlands. The Germans occupied the country

22
00:02:26.068 --> 00:02:33.498
for five years, starting in 1940. That sounds so frightening! AUDREY HEPBURN: It was! My dear

23
00:02:33.499 --> 00:02:42.385
uncle was executed by the Nazis; my brother Ian was forced to work in a German munitions factory; and Alexander went into hiding

24
00:02:42.386 --> 00:02:46.383
to avoid the same fate. I was among a group of

25
00:02:46.384 --> 00:02:51.608
girls rounded up to work in German kitchens. I managed to escape, but that's another

26
00:02:51.609 --> 00:02:55.599
story. FAYE MOSS: Is it true that you

27
00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:01.619
secretly worked against the Nazis as part of the Dutch Resistance? AUDREY HEPBURN: Oh, my role has

28
00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:09.301
been exaggerated over the years! Keep in mind that I was only 11 when the occupation started. I delivered a few messages and

29
00:03:09.302 --> 00:03:15.141
danced in clandestine recitals to benefit the Resistance. By the way, the audience never

30
00:03:15.142 --> 00:03:20.826
applauded for fear that the Nazis might discover us! FAYE MOSS: I've heard that many in

31
00:03:20.827 --> 00:03:25.720
the Netherlands were starving during the war. AUDREY HEPBURN: Sometimes my family was

32
00:03:25.721 --> 00:03:32.338
so desperate for food that we made flour out of tulip bulbs! When the war ended in

33
00:03:32.339 --> 00:03:39.284
1945, I was in poor health and suffering from malnutrition. A United Nations relief agency gave

34
00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:43.766
us food and clothing. I was so eager for a sweet

35
00:03:43.767 --> 00:03:50.093
treat that I drank a whole can of condensed milk at once! FAYE MOSS: Oh my! What happened to your

36
00:03:50.094 --> 00:03:54.586
family after the war? AUDREY HEPBURN: Amazingly both my brothers returned

37
00:03:54.587 --> 00:03:59.044
safely to us. After that I returned to

38
00:03:59.045 --> 00:04:01.995
dancing. My mother and I soon moved

39
00:04:01.996 --> 00:04:06.978
to Amsterdam so I could take ballet lessons. In 1948 we moved to London, again

40
00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:12.675
for my dancing. FAYE MOSS: How did you start acting?

41
00:04:12.676 --> 00:04:15.401
AUDREY HEPBURN: When my teacher

42
00:04:15.402 --> 00:04:24.167
told me I wasn't talented enough to be a professional ballerina, I joined the chorus line of a London musical. I later had a few

43
00:04:24.168 --> 00:04:29.902
bit parts in British movies and the lead in a French film. FAYE MOSS: This might be a

44
00:04:29.903 --> 00:04:34.700
good time to talk about your influence on fashion and beauty. You certainly looked different from

45
00:04:34.701 --> 00:04:39.574
most actresses in the 1950s. AUDREY HEPBURN: I was thin and

46
00:04:39.575 --> 00:04:43.723
had short hair and thick eyebrows. While most women wore

47
00:04:43.724 --> 00:04:50.320
dresses and heels, I preferred pants and flat shoes because I was tall. I also looked best in

48
00:04:50.321 --> 00:04:56.346
a limited range of colors: black, white, and beige. I didn't consider myself pretty, so

49
00:04:56.347 --> 00:05:02.754
I was surprised when women wanted "the Audrey Hepburn look." FAYE MOSS: And women still do today!

50
00:05:02.755 --> 00:05:05.984
Let's get back to your career.

51
00:05:05.985 --> 00:05:11.701
You came to the United States in 1951. AUDREY HEPBURN: Yes, I had the lead

52
00:05:11.702 --> 00:05:16.031
role in the Broadway play Gigi . My first Hollywood movie

53
00:05:16.032 --> 00:05:24.353
was Roman Holiday , which won me an Academy Award—or Oscar—in 1954. That same year I

54
00:05:24.354 --> 00:05:30.740
also earned a Tony Award for the play Ondine . FAYE MOSS: Suddenly you

55
00:05:30.741 --> 00:05:35.818
were an international star at age 24! You're probably most

56
00:05:35.819 --> 00:05:44.157
famous for playing the lead in the movie Breakfast at Tiffany's , but my favorite is My Fair Lady . AUDREY HEPBURN:

57
00:05:44.158 --> 00:05:48.758
I'm glad you like it, but that movie was a difficult project for me. Julie

58
00:05:48.759 --> 00:05:56.033
Andrews played the lead role on Broadway, so many people thought she should have gotten the movie role instead of me. To prove

59
00:05:56.034 --> 00:06:00.663
them wrong, I worked very hard to master the songs. I was terribly

60
00:06:00.664 --> 00:06:06.325
disappointed when another woman was later hired to dub my singing. FAYE MOSS: Oh no!

61
00:06:06.326 --> 00:06:14.766
Still, you were nominated for five Oscars during your career. Was that role one of them? AUDREY HEPBURN: No.

62
00:06:14.767 --> 00:06:20.738
Ironically Julie Andrews won that year for her role in Mary Poppins ! FAYE

63
00:06:20.739 --> 00:06:25.011
MOSS: You had pretty much stopped making movies by the late 1960s.

64
00:06:25.012 --> 00:06:28.450
AUDREY HEPBURN: Yes, I didn't like the parts I was

65
00:06:28.451 --> 00:06:36.422
being offered. By then I'd also started a family and wanted to devote more time to my two sons and home in Switzerland.

66
00:06:36.423 --> 00:06:42.488
FAYE MOSS: Yet you came out of retirement in the 1980s to serve as a goodwill ambassador for

67
00:06:42.489 --> 00:06:46.881
UNICEF, the United Nations agency that helps children and

68
00:06:46.882 --> 00:06:54.308
families. AUDREY HEPBURN: This work was so important to me because a forerunner of UNICEF had given me aid at the end of World War

69
00:06:54.309 --> 00:07:00.315
II. I knew what it was like to starve during wartime, and I'd always loved children.

70
00:07:00.316 --> 00:07:09.043
Starting in 1988 I traveled the world to raise money and awareness for children in need of food and medical care. FAYE

71
00:07:09.044 --> 00:07:15.626
MOSS: You've certainly led an interesting life, but I think it's time we let you return to history.

72
00:07:15.627 --> 00:07:17.701
Thank you, Audrey Hepburn! AUDREY

73
00:07:17.702 --> 00:07:23.451
HEPBURN: Thank you,

74
00:07:23.452 --> 00:07:32.486
Faye! FAYE MOSS: In December 1992 Audrey Hepburn received the Presidential Medal of Freedom, the highest civilian award in the

75
00:07:32.487 --> 00:07:42.630
United States. She died on January 20, 1993. The next day the first episode of her TV series, Gardens of the World

76
00:07:42.631 --> 00:07:50.225
, premiered. She was posthumously awarded an Emmy for that series, as well as a Grammy for an album of children's tales.

77
00:07:50.226 --> 00:07:54.165
And that's how she became one of the first EGOTs!

78
00:07:54.166 --> 00:08:00.179
That's all for today. See you next time on Superstars in History .

